  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 138 PM MST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nThe persistent upper-level ridge that has been parked over the \nregion, keeping above-normal temperatures and dry conditions in \nplace, is finally beginning to shift eastward today. This will \nestablish a more favorable storm track for approaching systems, \nessentially opening the door to a more active weather pattern \nheading into next week.\n\nThe first of what looks to be three separate disturbances \nexpected to move across the western CONUS this week can be seen on\nwater vapor imagery as an open trough moving through SoCal and \nArizona early this morning. As this approaches, we will see a \ntransition to southerly flow aloft over Colorado as the day \nprogresses. Today's afternoon high temperatures are on track to \nreach the low to mid 60s across the plains, outside of where \nmorning fog has limited the sun from heating things up so far \ntoday. Therefore, areas in our far northeastern corner and Lincoln\nCounty will be slightly cooler than our more densely populated \nareas along the urban corridor, where only some high cirrus are in\nplace. Our mountain valleys should see temperatures warm into the\nlow to mid 50s. \n\nMoisture is expected to increase overnight tonight as the trough \nnears, bringing an increase to cloud cover as the night progresses. \nAcross the lower elevations, fog is expected to develop late tonight \nthat may make for some hazardous driving conditions through the \nmorning on Saturday, and may lead to some impacts at DIA with \ndecreased arrival and departure rates possible for those with early \nmorning flights. Overnight low temperatures are forecast to drop \ninto the 30s across the plains, 20s for the higher elevations. \n\nLight mountain snow showers are expected to develop through the \nmorning tomorrow, with scattered rain showers forecast to move over \nthe plains for the afternoon and overnight as latest guidance shows \nthe trough closing off and becoming a closed low over southeastern \nColorado. Snow amounts are not expected to be very impressive with \nthis first round of moisture, but a few inches (1-3\") are expected \nfor elevations above 10,000 feet, with less than an inch forecast \nfor our mountain valleys. Areas over the Palmer Divide and into \nLincoln County will cool enough overnight Saturday that we may end \nup with some flakes falling before sunrise on Sunday, with the best \nchance for any light accumulations to be over the Palmer Divide. Fog \nis expected to develop again late Saturday night across Lincoln \nCounty before spreading northward across the eastern plains and \nalong the urban corridor, where it will gradually burn off through \nthe morning on Sunday. Outside of the morning fog, quiet weather is \nexpected to return across the forecast area for Sunday afternoon, \nwhere temperatures are expected to warm back up into the mid to \nupper 50s across the urban corridor.  \n\nA brief period of upper-level ridging is expected to return over the \nregion on Monday that will bring a slight warmup and drier \nconditions for the first few days of next week. By midweek, a second \ndisturbance is expected to approach the Intermountain West, though \nensemble guidance continues to show considerable spread and little \nconsensus. This is evident in all of the GEFS/AIFS-ENS/AIGEFS \nspaghetti plots, where 500mb height contours look more like an \nactual plate of spaghetti than a clear lock on any particular \noutcome. The same can be said for the third potential disturbance \nthat may arrive by the end of next week. While confidence remains \nlow at this time, this system has the potential to be the most \nimpactful of the three, bringing a more substantial cooldown and \nperhaps even our first accumulating snowfall across the lower \nelevations. There is still much to unpack, so stay tuned!",
